# {{ORG_NAME}} — session preamble

{{PLUGIN_NAME}} is {{ORG_NAME}}'s agent harness. This file is a **pointer**, not the
process: the operating model lives in the org brain (`{{ORG_WIKI_NAME}}`) and is read at
runtime, so it can change without re-emitting this configuration.

- **Run `/{{VERB_PRIME}}` first, every session.** It loads the operating model, the wiki
  schema, the compliance guardrails, and the relevant decisions from
  `{{ORG_WIKI_NAME}}`, then tells you in a few lines what process is in force.
- **The brain is at** `${{ORG_WIKI_PATH_ENV}}` (falling back to
  `{{ORG_WIKI_DEFAULT_PATH}}`). If the two ever disagree with this file, the wiki wins.
- **New here?** `/{{VERB_INTRO}}` is the one-screen orientation.
- **Model floor ({{ORG_NAME}}):** default **{{MODEL_POLICY_DEFAULT}}**; banned:
  **{{MODEL_POLICY_BANNED}}**. {{MODEL_POLICY_RULE}}
- **One provider.** `opencode.json` carries
  `"enabled_providers": ["{{OC_PROVIDER_ID}}"]` — an **allowlist**, so only
  `{{OC_PROVIDER_ID}}` is offered even on a machine with an ambient
  `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` / `OPENAI_API_KEY` that opencode would otherwise auto-detect as a
  provider. (`disabled_providers` is kept alongside it; a deny-list is the weaker control
  because it only covers providers someone thought to name.)
- **The graph is states one agent traverses, not a cast (ADR 0018, extending ADR 0017).**
  Within a task the graph is one general `{{OC_PRIMARY_AGENT}}` graph-agent that walks
  understand → build → validate → **review (self-check)** → fix → clear in a **single
  context**, changing skill/rubric/effort per node. Review is a *mode* of that one agent,
  not a separately-spawned validator. You are the **orchestrator**: you fan out one build
  graph-agent **worker** per task and hold its conclusions, never its transcript.
    - **Writers → `dispatch`** — one call launches one `{{OC_PRIMARY_AGENT}}` graph-agent
      that traverses the whole build → review → fix loop for one task. `dispatch` gives
      the per-(repo, ticket) worktree a native child cannot (a native subagent child never
      relocates from the parent's location).
    - **The relied-upon independent review is the human merge gate + CI** — never a second
      agent inside the loop. Fan out several workers only for genuinely-independent tasks.
- **`dispatch` is a thin launcher** (`plugin/dispatch.js`): `dispatch({ agent, ticket,
  repo?, model?, variant?, task_id?, command? })`. It creates a worker as a **root
  session** at a git worktree of `repo` (one per (repo, ticket)) running the graph-agent
  you name, then **returns the `task_id` at once** — non-blocking. The worker's
  `<run-closed>` result posts back with `resume:false` and surfaces on your **next turn**
  (poll-free; no autonomous loop). Because workers are roots, the **native session list is
  your fleet view** — no custom UI. Call it several times in one turn to fan out
  genuinely-independent tasks concurrently; pass `task_id` to continue a worker. It fails
  closed on a missing agent, neither ticket nor task_id, an ambiguous/escaping repo, or a
  banned/off-provider model. The `task` permission rule denies `*` and allowlists exactly
  the spawnable set, so a typo'd `subagent_type` is denied, never silently the
  full-permission primary agent.
{{#SUPP_REVIEWER_ENABLED}}
- **Optional supplementary reviewer** (`agent/validate.md`, `subagent_type: "validate"`):
  for a **high-risk diff only**, a fresh-context, read-only pass over a **completed** PR —
  it reviews the finished artifact and does not interleave inside the build → review → fix
  loop. It is read-only by its own frontmatter (denies `{{OC_VALIDATE_DENY_LIST}}`; the
  native tool derives the child session's permissions from it), and the `task` rule
  allowlists it (`{{OC_PRIMARY_AGENT}}` + `validate`). This is the exception, not the
  standard graph.
{{/SUPP_REVIEWER_ENABLED}}
- **`plugin/reminders.js` is advisory only**: a toast at session start (run
  `/{{VERB_PRIME}}`) and the handoff line injected at compaction. It never blocks or edits.
  The compaction handoff runs on every supported host (opencode 1.18.29+ and 2.x); the
  toast nudge is the 1.x half — 2.x has no server-side toast.
- **`plugin/wiki-pull.js` is advisory only**: on session start it fast-forwards the local
  org brain (`${{ORG_WIKI_PATH_ENV}}`) to `origin/main` when that clone is on `main` and
  clean, so `/{{VERB_PRIME}}` reads current decisions. It never merges, never touches a
  dirty tree, and fails open — a fetch failure can never break a session.

Everything else — the verbs, the build graph-agent's graph, the gates — is described
where it lives: `command/` and `skill/`{{#SUPP_REVIEWER_ENABLED}}, with the optional
supplementary reviewer's contract in `agent/validate.md`{{/SUPP_REVIEWER_ENABLED}}.
